WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.939
I’m off to the Arctic for three weeks.

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:05.190
And while I’m gone, some guest presenters
are going to be taking over this channel.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.450
We start with Sally le Page,

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.660
a biologist from Oxford University,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.330
who is going to be talking about the world’s
longest lasting battery.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.210
Be nice, be kind, I’ll see you in three
weeks.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.210
Take it away, Sally!

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.310
I’m in the Clarendon lab in Oxford,

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:23.070
where physicists keep a battery with the longest
battery life the world has ever seen.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.910
It powers the Oxford Electric Bell,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:30.310
and it’s been running almost continuously
for over 176 years.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.929
It was bought by the Reverend Robert Walker,

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:37.289
who was a reader in experimental philosophy
at Oxford.

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:39.550
That’s what they used to call physicists
in those days,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.309
and should hopefully give you an idea of quite
how long ago we’re talking.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.890
He brought it back in 1840.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.609
So let’s just take a second to put that
in some context.

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:53.319
If we skip back another 60 years, the year
is 1780.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.030
Mad King George is still on the British throne,

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.039
the US declared independence just four years
earlier,

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:01.229
and Napoleon is still a schoolboy.

00:01:01.229 --> 00:01:04.489
Over in Italy, a physicist, Luigi Galvani,

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.140
was dissecting a frog’s leg with metal tools,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.000
but when he touched the nerve, he saw the
frog’s muscles twitch

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.840
and he thought that this was “animal electricity”.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.810
Another Italian physicist, Alessandro Volta,
heard of this experiment,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.119
and realised that the frog’s leg was just
a fancy electrolyte

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:26.700
that allowed current to flow between the copper
and iron tools that Galvani was using.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.479
And Volta went on to invent the first ever
battery in 1800,

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:35.599
called the voltaic pile, made by piling up
discs of copper and zinc

00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:37.799
— well, here I’ve got aluminium foil —

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:42.560
and sandwiching them with discs of cardboard
that was soaked in brine.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.259
And by piling up cells like this,

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:48.340
you can see that you produce a battery, and
it still works!

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:54.899
And then in 1825, the London instrument makers
Watkin and Hill made this:

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:58.789
the Oxford Electric Bell and the dry pile
battery that powers it.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:05.740
And although it was just 25 years after Volta
invented the first ever battery,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:12.050
this battery here went on to outlive every
single other battery the world has ever produced

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.660
and has won the Guinness World Record for
the world’s most durable battery.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.340
How does such an old battery last so long?

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.410
Well, it’s a dry pile battery, so it’s
got a paste inside

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.010
with the minimum amount of water needed for
the electrolyte to work.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.480
And all that water is kept in with this solid
sulfur coating.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.450
But beyond that, we don’t really know what’s
inside it.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.340
Looking at diagrams of other batteries from
around the time,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:42.070
it’s probably got about 2000 of these discs
made from zinc and manganese dioxide all stacked up.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.790
But to find out what it’s exactly made of,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:49.350
we’d have to cut it open and end its 176-year
run.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.670
In this time, the bells have run around 10
billion times, give or take,

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.940
although this is an active physics laboratory,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.140
so thankfully for all the physicists around
here

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.850
it’s now inaduble and it’s behind two
panes of glass.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:06.920
Each time the bell rings, the clapper takes
a tiny one nanoamp of current,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.740
but the voltage between the bells is a whopping
2 kilovolts.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:15.310
That’s nearly 10 times the voltage of UK
mains electricity.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.360
How long can it keep on running for?

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.930
Well, no one really knows.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.580
The people round here reckon that the bell
will break from wear and tear

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.760
long before the battery runs out of power.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.200
In 1841, just one year after Oxford bought
the bell,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.380
the instrument makers themselves wrote in
a letter,

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.310
“The residual electrical power sufficient
to keep up the ringing of the bells

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.090
“seldom lasts more than three or four years.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.530
“It’s a pretty apparatus, but alas,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.790
“very transient in its working powers.”

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.160
How wrong could they be?

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.950
That was Sally le Page, she has some great
videos on her channel,

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.240
check it out — links are on screen or in
the description now.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.650
Thank you also to the University of Oxford’s
physics department for letting us film the bell.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.990
Next time: we have someone with some teeth
dissolving in soda.

